{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Lex now supports resource-based policies for bots and aliases","context":"Amazon Lex, a service for creating conversational interfaces, now supports resource-based policies for managing access permissions to bots and aliases. Previously, access permissions were managed using identity-based policies, which required defining and managing policies for each IAM user or role. Resource-based policies allow attaching a single policy to a Lex bot or alias, enabling management of permissions for multiple IAM users, roles, or AWS services","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2021/05/amazon-lex-now-supports-resource-based-policies-for-bots-and-aliases/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS introduces additional policy details to access denied error messages - AWS","context":"AWS has updated access denied error messages in scenarios where the resources are within the same account and organization. The new error messages include the Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the specific IAM and AWS Organizations policy responsible for the denied access. This enhancement allows for faster identification and resolution of access denied issues, particularly in cases where multiple policies of the same type are involved. The error message now provides the ARN for various policy types, including Service Control Policies (SCP), Resource Control Policies (RCP), identity-based policies, session policies, and permission boundaries. This new feature will be rolled out across all AWS services and regions. For more information, refer to the IAM documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/01/additional-policy-details-access-denied-error/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS IAM Identity Center Identity Store APIs now accept resource ARNs in addition to resource IDs - AWS","context":"AWS IAM Identity Center has updated its Identity Store APIs to accept Amazon Resource Names (ARNs) in addition to resource IDs for users, groups, group memberships, and identity stores. This change simplifies application code and eliminates potential parsing errors. ARN support is additive and existing integrations continue to work. The change applies to every request identifier field across the Identity Store API surface. Responses continue to return resource IDs as before. Malformed or wrong-resource-type ARNs will return a ValidationException. This capability is available in all AWS Regions where AWS IAM Identity Center is offered, at no additional cost. For more information, refer to the Identity Store API Reference","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/iam-identity-center-apis-arns/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-iam","skill_description":"Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.\n\n\nServices: iam, sts, organizations\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure, policy-generation, permissions, source-code-to-policy, terraform-plan-to-policy\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, security-engineer\nWorkload: serverless, containers, data-analytics, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-iam"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"waf","skill_description":"Configures AWS WAF to filter web traffic: creating web access control lists (web ACLs) on CloudFront, Application Load Balancers, API Gateway, and AppSync; AWS Managed Rules tuned in Count mode; rate-based rules for HTTP floods; IP set and geographic match rules; Bot Control (Common and Targeted); turning bot labels into a confidence signal; stripping spoofed inbound x-amzn-waf-* headers; recovering the real client IP behind a CDN; Fraud Control (account takeover and account creation fraud prevention); and logging and request sampling. Use when the user wants to protect a web application or API from common exploits, bots, credential stuffing, fake-account creation, or HTTP floods at the application layer (layer 7). Routes to the right per-task procedure in references. Do NOT use for L3/L4 DDoS protection (shieldadvanced skill), multi-account WAF rollout (firewallmanager skill), CloudFront configuration (cloudfront skill), or Route 53 health checks or records (route53 skill).\n\n\nServices: waf, wafv2, cloudfront, elb, apigateway, appsync\nTasks: configure, secure, debug\nPersona: developer, devops, security-engineer\nWorkload: security, web-application\n*","skill_name":"waf"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking AWS docs on Lex V2 RecognizeText IAM resource ARN requirements"}

{"id": "ef1032db-8b4c-498b-b83f-365509d53e32", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Right, so RecognizeText calls operate on a bot-alias ARN, but the policy I'm checking only grants permission on the bot ARN itself, not the bot-alias—that's the gap causing the denial.

Given no explicit Deny statements exist, this falls to implicit deny: the request's actual resource (the bot-alias ARN) simply doesn't match the Allow statement's resource (the bot ARN). The fix is to update the Resource field in the identity policy to include the bot-alias ARN format so it actually covers what RecognizeText evaluates against.

